Experiment 1: Linux Basics, Redirection & Process Management
Directory & File Operations: Essential command syntax and options for navigating (pwd, cd), managing directories (mkdir -p/-v/-m, rmdir -p/-v), listing metadata (ls -lah), creating/updating files (touch -c/-t/-d), copying (cp -r/-i/-p), moving/renaming (mv -i/-n/-v), deleting (rm -rf/-i), viewing contents (cat -n/-b), and extracting line ranges (head, tail).
I/O Redirection & Streams: Understanding standard streams (stdin 0, stdout 1, stderr 2), overwriting (>) vs. appending (>>), redirecting errors (2>), combining output and error streams (&>), and feeding file input (<).
Pipes & Utilities: Stream chaining via pipes (|), alphabetical sorting (sort), deduplication (uniq), splitting output streams (tee), and converting stream inputs to command-line arguments (xargs).
Pattern Searching (grep) & Finding Files (find): Text search flags (-i, -v, -c, -w, -n) alongside disk-wide searches filtering by size (-size), name/case (-name, -iname), user ownership (-user), and empty paths (-empty).
Terminal Text Editors: Workflow modes, search, line management, and exit operations in modal (vim) and modeless (nano) environments.
Process Management: Shell job control (jobs, bg, fg), process inspection (ps, ps -e, ps -ef, ps -p, ps aux), signal handling (kill -15 SIGTERM, kill -9 SIGKILL, kill -STOP, kill -CONT), CPU priority scheduling (nice, renice), and live diagnostic monitoring (top).
Theory:
Directory Operations
pwd (Print Working Directory)
● Description: Prints the full absolute path from the root directory to the current working
location.
● Syntax: pwd [OPTION]
● Example: pwd
● Output: /home/ubuntu
cd (Change Directory)
● Description: Navigates the terminal session to a different directory in the filesystem.
● Syntax: cd [DIRECTORY]
● System Example: cd /etc/netplan
● Key Shortcuts:
○ Move up one level: cd ..
○ Previous directory: cd -
○ Return to Home Directory (using shortcut): cd ~ (or simply cd)
○ Return to Home Directory (without shortcut): cd /home/ubuntu (or cd $HOME)

mkdir (Make Directory)
● Description: Creates one or more new directories in the filesystem.
● Syntax: mkdir [OPTION] DIRECTORY...
● Standard Creation:
○ Using ~: mkdir ~/scripts
○ Without ~: mkdir /home/ubuntu/scripts
● Useful Options:
○ -p (Parents): Creates intermediate directories automatically.
■ Using ~: mkdir -p ~/Documents/projects/app/src
■ Without ~: mkdir -p /home/ubuntu/Documents/projects/app/src
○ -v (Verbose): Prints confirmation for every created directory.
■ Using ~: mkdir -v ~/Downloads/iso

■ Without ~: mkdir -v /home/ubuntu/Downloads/iso
○ -m (Mode): Sets octal permissions upon creation.
■ Using ~: mkdir -m 700 ~/.ssh
■ Without ~: mkdir -m 700 /home/ubuntu/.ssh
● Modifying Permissions of an Existing Folder using chmod:
○ Using ~: chmod 755 ~/Documents/projects
○ Without ~: chmod 755 /home/ubuntu/Documents/projects

rmdir (Remove Directory)
● Description: Deletes empty directories safely.
● Syntax: rmdir [OPTION] DIRECTORY...
● Standard Removal:
○ Using ~: rmdir ~/Templates
○ Without ~: rmdir /home/ubuntu/Templates
● Useful Options:
○ -p (Parents): Removes empty directory chains.
■ Using ~: rmdir -p ~/Documents/projects/app/src
■ Without ~: rmdir -p /home/ubuntu/Documents/projects/app/src
○ -v (Verbose): Confirms deletion.
■ Using ~: rmdir -v ~/Downloads/iso
■ Without ~: rmdir -v /home/ubuntu/Downloads/iso

2. File Operations
ls (List Directory Contents)
● Description: Lists directory contents and metadata.
● Syntax: ls [OPTION]... [FILE]...
● Long Listing of Home (including hidden dotfiles):
○ Using ~: ls -lah ~
○ Without ~: ls -lah /home/ubuntu
● Useful Options:
○ -l (Long format): Displays file permissions, owner, group, size, and timestamp.
○ -a (All): Shows hidden configuration files (e.g., .bashrc, .profile).
○ -h (Human-readable): Renders file sizes in KB, MB, or GB.
○ -t (Time): Sorts contents by modification date, newest first.
○ -R (Recursive): Lists files across all nested subdirectories.

touch
● Description: Creates an empty file or updates the access/modification timestamps of an
existing file.
● Syntax: touch [OPTION]... FILE...
● Standard Creation:
○ Using ~: touch ~/.bash_aliases
○ Without ~: touch /home/ubuntu/.bash_aliases
● Useful Options:
○ -c (No create): Refreshes timestamp only if the file exists.
■ Using ~: touch -c ~/.bashrc
■ Without ~: touch -c /home/ubuntu/.bashrc
○ -t (Custom timestamp): Formats as [[CC]YY]MMDDhhmm[.ss].
■ Using ~: touch -t 202601011200 ~/install.log
■ Without ~: touch -t 202601011200 /home/ubuntu/install.log
○ -d (Date string): Accepts flexible human-readable dates.
■ Using ~: touch -d &quot;2026-04-23&quot; ~/release_notes.txt
■ Without ~: touch -d &quot;2026-04-23&quot; /home/ubuntu/release_notes.txt
■ Using ~: touch -d &quot;09:00&quot; ~/.bash_aliases
■ Without ~: touch -d &quot;09:00&quot; /home/ubuntu/.bash_aliases

cp (Copy)
● Description: Copies files and directories from source to destination.
● Syntax: cp [OPTION]... SOURCE... DEST
● Standard Copy:
○ Using ~: cp /etc/skel/.bashrc ~/.bashrc.bak
○ Without ~: cp /etc/skel/.bashrc /home/ubuntu/.bashrc.bak
● Useful Options:
○ -r / -R (Recursive): Copies full directory trees.
■ Using ~: cp -r /etc/netplan ~/backup_netplan
■ Without ~: cp -r /etc/netplan /home/ubuntu/backup_netplan
○ -i (Interactive): Prompts before overwriting existing files.
■ Using ~: cp -i /etc/hosts ~/hosts
■ Without ~: cp -i /etc/hosts /home/ubuntu/hosts
○ -p (Preserve): Retains modes, ownership, and timestamps.
■ Using ~: cp -p /etc/timezone ~/timezone_backup
■ Without ~: cp -p /etc/timezone /home/ubuntu/timezone_backup

mv (Move / Rename)

● Description: Moves files or directories to new locations or renames them.
● Syntax: mv [OPTION]... SOURCE... DEST
● Standard Move / Rename:
○ Using ~: mv ~/Desktop ~/Workspace
○ Without ~: mv /home/ubuntu/Desktop /home/ubuntu/Workspace
● Useful Options:
○ -i (Interactive): Prompts before overwriting destination files.
■ Using ~: mv -i ~/test.conf /etc/netplan/
■ Without ~: mv -i /home/ubuntu/test.conf /etc/netplan/
○ -n (No clobber): Prevents overwriting silently.
■ Using ~: mv -n ~/.bashrc.bak ~/.bashrc
■ Without ~: mv -n /home/ubuntu/.bashrc.bak /home/ubuntu/.bashrc
○ -v (Verbose): Prints names of moved items.
■ Using ~: mv -v ~/Downloads/*.deb ~/Packages/
■ Without ~: mv -v /home/ubuntu/Downloads/*.deb /home/ubuntu/Packages/

rm (Remove)
● Description: Permanently deletes files and directories.
● Syntax: rm [OPTION]... FILE...
● System Directory Example: rm -rf /tmp/apt-* (Targets global system temp folder; no ~
applicable)
● Useful Options:
○ -r (Recursive): Deletes directories and their contents.
■ Using ~: rm -r ~/Downloads/temp_setup/
■ Without ~: rm -r /home/ubuntu/Downloads/temp_setup/
○ -f (Force): Suppresses prompts and missing-file errors.
■ System path: rm -f /tmp/lockfile
○ -i (Interactive): Asks confirmation before each deletion.
■ Using ~: rm -i ~/.bash_history
■ Without ~: rm -i /home/ubuntu/.bash_history

cat (Concatenate)
● Description: Outputs entire file contents to standard output.
● Syntax: cat [OPTION]... [FILE]...
● System Example: cat /etc/os-release
● Useful Options:
○ -n (Number): Numbers every line of output.
■ Example: cat -n /etc/shells

○ -b (Number non-blank): Numbers non-empty lines only.
■ Example: cat -b /etc/issue

head &amp; tail
● Description: Displays the beginning or end of files and input streams.
● Syntax: head -n [N] FILE | tail -n [N] FILE
● System Inspection Examples:
○ First 10 user accounts: head -n 10 /etc/passwd
○ First 5 filesystem mounts: head -n 5 /etc/fstab
○ Last 25 auth logs: tail -n 25 /var/log/auth.log
○ Last 50 package install logs: tail -n 50 /var/log/dpkg.log
GREP (Global Regular Expression Print)
Terminal-based search tool functioning like Ctrl+F:
● Case 1 (Ignoring case sensitivity -i): grep -i &quot;error&quot; logs.txt

● Case 2 (Inverting matches -v): grep -v &quot;error&quot; logs.txt (excludes search terms)
● Case 3 (Counting lines matching a profession -c): grep -c &quot;Doctor&quot; users.csv
● Case 4 (Isolating exact words -w): grep -w &quot;Carly&quot; users.csv
● Case 5 (Pinpointing line numbers -n): grep -n &quot;Carly&quot; users.csv
1. jobs
● What is jobs? Displays the jobs managed by the current shell, including jobs that are running
in the background or stopped.

● Why do we use it? When you start several programs from one terminal (sleep 100 &amp;, sleep
200 &amp;, sleep 300 &amp;), jobs answers: &quot;Which jobs that I started from this terminal are still
running?&quot;
● Example:
sleep 100 &amp;
jobs
Possible output: [1]+ Running sleep 100 &amp;
○ [1] → Job ID
○ Running → Job status
○ sleep 100 → Command
● Stopped-job example:
Run sleep 100, then press Ctrl + Z. Output: [1]+ Stopped sleep 100. Now jobs shows the
stopped job.
● Job ID vs PID:
In [1] 6721: 1 is Job ID (used by shell: fg %1, bg %1), 6721 is PID.
● Real-life use: Running multiple background tasks and needing to quickly see their status
without searching through the entire system process list.
● Remember: jobs = Show jobs managed by my current shell.
2. bg
● What is bg? bg means background. It resumes a stopped job and lets it continue running in
the background.
● Why do we use it? When you temporarily stop a program with Ctrl + Z, you want it to
continue running while giving your terminal back.
● Example:
Start sleep 100 → Press Ctrl + Z → jobs shows [1]+ Stopped sleep 100 → Run bg → jobs
shows [1]+ Running sleep 100 &amp;.
● Specific job: If job 2 is stopped: bg %2
● Process flow: Running → Ctrl + Z → Stopped → bg → Running in background.
● Remember: bg = Continue a stopped job in the background.
3. fg
● What is fg? fg means foreground. It brings a background or stopped job to the foreground.
● Why do we use it? When a program is running in the background but you now want to
interact with it through your terminal.
● Example: sleep 100 &amp; → Check jobs → Run fg → The sleep job becomes the
foreground job.
● Specific job: fg %2 brings job 2 to foreground.

● Relationship: Background → fg → Foreground.
● Remember: fg = Bring a job to the foreground.
4. ps
● What is ps? ps stands for Process Status. It displays information about processes.
● Why do we use it? When you want to know: &quot;What processes are associated with my current
session?&quot;
● Example: Run ps.
Possible output:
PID TTY TIME CMD
5025 pts/0 00:00:00 bash
6721 pts/0 00:00:00 sleep
6764 pts/0 00:00:00 ps
● Important columns:
○ PID: Process ID
○ TTY: Terminal associated with process
○ TIME: CPU time used
○ CMD: Command
● Important observation: 5025 pts/0 bash means Bash is running as process 5025 and is
associated with terminal pts/0.
● Why is PID important? You can use it to manage a process: kill 6721.
● Remember: ps = View process information.
5. ps -e
● What is it? ps -e shows all processes on the system.
● Why do we use it? ps gives a limited session view, but Linux runs system services, network
services, kernel processes, applications, and shells.
● Example: Run ps -e.
Possible output:
1 ? 00:00:05 systemd
2 ? 00:00:00 kthreadd
500 ? 00:00:00 NetworkManager
5025 pts/0 00:00:00 bash
● Why so many processes? Linux uses processes for system/service management (systemd),
networking (NetworkManager), SSH (sshd), shell (bash), and applications (firefox).
● Remember: ps -e = Show all processes.
6. ps -ef

● What is it? ps -ef shows all processes in full-format detail.
● Why do we use it? To discover process owner, PID, parent PID (PPID), start time, and
launching command.
● Example:
UID PID PPID C STIME TTY TIME CMD
root 1 0 0 20:00 ? 00:00:03 /sbin/init
root 500 1 0 20:00 ? 00:00:00 sshd
vboxuser 5025 500 0 21:00 pts/0 00:00:00 bash
vboxuser 6721 5025 0 21:10 pts/0 00:00:00 sleep 100
● Important columns:
○ UID: User who owns the process (root, vboxuser).
○ PID: Process ID (e.g., 6721).
○ PPID: Parent Process ID. If PID 6721 has PPID 5025, bash (5025) spawned sleep
(6721).
○ C: CPU-related scheduling/accounting field.
○ STIME: Process start time/date.
○ TTY: Terminal associated (pts/0, pts/1, ?).
○ TIME: CPU time consumed.
○ CMD: Command used to start the process.
● Real-life use: Inspecting unfamiliar process parentage: ps -p 5025 -f.
● Remember: ps -ef = All processes + detailed information + parent/child relationship.
7. ps -p PID
● What is it? Select a process by PID.
● Syntax: ps -p PID
● Example: ps -p 6721
● Why do we use it? Inspect one specific process and verify its identity before acting (e.g.,
before kill 6721).
● Detailed form: ps -p 6721 -f
● Remember: ps -p PID = Check a specific process using its PID.
8. ps -o pid,ni,cmd
● What is it? Choose exactly which columns to display.
● Meaning: pid = Process ID, ni = Nice value, cmd = Command.
● Example output:
PID NI CMD
5025 0 bash
7100 0 sleep 300
7150 10 sleep 300

● Why useful? Directly compare scheduling priority between normal process (NI = 0) and nice
process (NI = 10).
● Remember: ps -o = Customize which process information is displayed.
9. ps aux
● What is it? Shows processes with information particularly useful for resource monitoring.
● Why do we use it? Answer: &quot;Which process is consuming CPU or memory?&quot;
● Important columns: USER, PID, %CPU, %MEM, STAT, COMMAND.
● Example:
vboxuser 5200 95.0 2.0 R python3
vboxuser 5300 3.0 25.0 S firefox
● Important distinction:
○ ps -ef → detailed process information / relationships.
○ ps aux → CPU and memory/resource information.
● Remember: ps aux = View processes with CPU and memory usage.
10. kill
● What is kill? Sends a signal to a process (does not simply mean &quot;destroy a process&quot;).
● Syntax: kill PID (e.g., kill 6721). By default sends SIGTERM (signal 15).
● Why do we use it? To control a process using signals.
● Basic flow: Find process → Get PID → kill PID → Send SIGTERM → Process
terminates normally.
● Remember: kill = Send a signal to a process.
11. kill -15 PID
● What is signal 15? SIGTERM (Please terminate normally).
● Why do we use it? Gives the application an opportunity to clean up before exiting (close files,
save data, close connections, clean up, exit).
● Note: kill PID and kill -15 PID are normally equivalent.
● Remember: SIGTERM = Graceful termination request.
12. kill -9 PID
● What is signal 9? SIGKILL. Forces immediate termination.
● Why do we use it? When a process doesn&#39;t respond to normal termination. SIGKILL cannot
be caught, blocked, or ignored.
● Difference: kill -15 = &quot;Please terminate&quot;; kill -9 = &quot;Terminate immediately&quot;.
● Correct workflow: kill PID → Doesn&#39;t terminate? → kill -9 PID.

● Caution: Application doesn&#39;t get an opportunity to perform cleanup.
● Remember: kill -9 = Forcefully terminate a process.
13. kill -l
● What is it? Lists available signals.
● Key signals:
○ 1: SIGHUP (Hangup)
○ 2: SIGINT (Interrupt)
○ 9: SIGKILL (Force termination)
○ 15: SIGTERM (Graceful termination)
○ 18: SIGCONT (Continue)
○ 19: SIGSTOP (Stop)
● Remember: kill -l = List available signals.
14. kill -STOP PID
● What is it? Sends SIGSTOP to suspend a running process without terminating it.
● Example: kill -STOP 7000. Flow: RUNNING → SIGSTOP → STOPPED. Process still exists
in memory.
● Important Lesson: Accidental execution on Bash shell (e.g., kill -STOP 4345) freezes
terminal responsiveness.
● Remember: SIGSTOP = Stop/pause a process.
15. kill -CONT PID
● What is it? Sends SIGCONT to continue a process that has been stopped.
● Example: kill -CONT 7000. Flow: STOPPED → SIGCONT → RUNNING.
● Remember: SIGCONT = Continue a stopped process.
16. nice
● What is nice? Starts a process with a specified nice value, influencing its CPU scheduling
priority.
● Why do we use it? Deprioritize CPU-heavy jobs relative to interactive processes.
● Nice value range: -20 (more favorable) to 0 (default) to +19 (less favorable).
○ Lower NI → more favorable scheduling; Higher NI → less favorable
scheduling.

● Experiment: nice -n 10 sleep 300 &amp; vs sleep 300 &amp;. Check with ps -o pid,ni,cmd.
● Misconception: nice -n 10 does not mean run at 10% CPU; it sets scheduling priority weight.
● Remember: nice = Start a process with a chosen nice value.

17. renice
● What is renice? Changes the nice value of an already running process.
● Syntax: renice 10 -p PID.
● nice vs renice:
○ nice: Set nice value when starting a process.
○ renice: Change nice value of an existing process.
● Remember: renice = Change the nice value of an already-running process.
18. top
● What is top? Live, continuously updating view of system processes and resource usage.
● Why top instead of ps? ps aux is a photograph/snapshot; top is live video with constant
updates.
● Interactive commands inside top:
○ q: Exit top.
○ k: Kill a process (prompts for PID and signal).
○ c: Toggle short command name vs full command line.
○ u: Filter processes by username.
○ f: Manage fields/columns displayed.
○ z: Toggle color display.
○ b: Toggle highlighting of running processes.
● Remember: top = Live monitoring of processes and system resources.

9. The Complete Process-Management Picture
● Job Control:
○ jobs → See shell jobs
○ bg → Continue stopped job in background
○ fg → Bring job to foreground
● Process Inspection:
○ ps → Current session processes
○ ps -e → All processes
○ ps -ef → Detailed process information with PPID
○ ps -p PID → One specific process
○ ps aux → Processes + CPU/RAM information
● Process Control:
○ kill PID → SIGTERM (graceful)
○ kill -15 PID → Graceful termination

○ kill -9 PID → Forceful termination
○ kill -STOP PID → Stop/pause
○ kill -CONT PID → Continue
○ kill -l → List signals
● Process Priority:
○ nice → Set priority when starting
○ renice → Change priority after starting
● Live Monitoring:
○ top → Continuously monitor processes
